Minimal write-capable Tool plugin for drupal/mcp_server 2.x on mcp/sdk 0.6. File: `<module>/src/Plugin/Tool/UpdateNoteTool.php`. Discovery is attribute-based (`#[Tool]` under `src/Plugin/Tool/`); run `drush cr` after adding it.

```php
<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

namespace Drupal\my_module\Plugin\Tool;

use Drupal\Core\Access\AccessResult;
use Drupal\Core\Access\AccessResultInterface;
use Drupal\Core\Session\AccountInterface;
use Drupal\Core\StringTranslation\TranslatableMarkup;
use Drupal\mcp_server\Attribute\Tool;
use Drupal\mcp_server\Plugin\ToolPluginBase;
use Mcp\Server\ClientGateway;

#[Tool(
  id: 'update_note',                       // plugin ID = MCP wire name; no ':'
  label: new TranslatableMarkup('Update note'),
  description: new TranslatableMarkup('Updates the body of a note entity by ID.'),
  inputSchema: [
    'type' => 'object',
    'properties' => [
      'id' => ['type' => 'integer'],
      'body' => ['type' => 'string'],
    ],
    'required' => ['id', 'body'],
  ],
  readOnly: FALSE,
  destructive: TRUE,                       // client hints only — not enforcement
  idempotent: TRUE,
)]
final class UpdateNoteTool extends ToolPluginBase {

  // Native plugins default to enabled=FALSE — without this the tool is
  // discovered but never appears in tools/list.
  protected function defaultConfiguration(): array {
    return ['enabled' => TRUE];
  }

  // Declared on ToolPluginInterface but NOT auto-enforced (see below).
  public function checkAccess(AccountInterface $account): AccessResultInterface {
    return AccessResult::allowedIfHasPermission($account, 'update notes via mcp');
  }

  // 0.6 SDK handler contract (ToolHandlerInterface) — not defined on the base
  // class; you implement it. $arguments is already validated against inputSchema.
  public function execute(array $arguments, ClientGateway $gateway): mixed {
    // ... load entity $arguments['id'], set body, save ...
    return ['success' => TRUE];
  }

}
```

Gating — the #1 gotcha: core calls nothing on the `tools/call` path, so overriding `checkAccess()` alone does nothing; any client reaching the endpoint can call any enabled tool. To enforce it, register an event subscriber on the SDK's `Mcp\Event\RequestEvent` that resolves the plugin from the `CallToolRequest` name and throws to deny:

```php
public function onRequest(RequestEvent $event): void {
  $request = $event->getRequest();
  if (!$request instanceof CallToolRequest) {
    return;
  }
  $plugin = $this->toolManager->createInstance($request->name);
  if (!$plugin->checkToolAccess($request->name, $this->currentUser)->isAllowed()) {
    throw new McpAuthorizationDeniedException('forbidden', 403);
  }
}
```

(`McpAuthorizationDeniedException` is `Drupal\mcp_server\Exception\...`; the controller maps it to HTTP 401/403. Register the subscriber in services.yml with `@plugin.manager.mcp_server.tool` and `@current_user`, tagged `event_subscriber`. Alternatively enable the `mcp_server_oauth` companion for OAuth2 scope gating. Ideally also filter `ListToolsRequest` so ungated clients don't see the write tool's name.)

