declare(strict_types=1);

namespace Drupal\my_module\Plugin\Tool;

use Drupal\Core\Access\AccessResult;
use Drupal\Core\Access\AccessResultInterface;
use Drupal\Core\Session\AccountInterface;
use Drupal\Core\StringTranslation\TranslatableMarkup;
use Drupal\mcp_server\Attribute\Tool;
use Drupal\mcp_server\Plugin\ToolPluginBase;
use Mcp\Server\ClientGateway;

#[Tool(
  id: 'update_note',
  label: new TranslatableMarkup('Update note'),
  description: new TranslatableMarkup('Updates the body of a note entity by ID.'),
  inputSchema: [
    'type' => 'object',
    'properties' => [
      'id' => ['type' => 'string', 'description' => 'Note ID.'],
      'body' => ['type' => 'string', 'description' => 'New body text.'],
    ],
    'required' => ['id', 'body'],
  ],
  // Honest client hints for a write tool (not enforcement):
  readOnly: FALSE,
  destructive: TRUE,   // the default, stated explicitly
  idempotent: FALSE,
  openWorld: FALSE,
)]
final class UpdateNoteTool extends ToolPluginBase {

  // Required: native plugins default to enabled=FALSE and never register.
  protected function defaultConfiguration(): array {
    return ['enabled' => TRUE];
  }

  // The 0.6 handler contract (from the SDK's ToolHandlerInterface) —
  // NOT defined on ToolPluginBase; you implement it.
  public function execute(array $arguments, ClientGateway $gateway): mixed {
    try {
      // ... load entity by $arguments['id'], set body, save ...
      return ['success' => TRUE, 'data' => ['id' => $arguments['id']]];
    }
    catch (\Throwable $e) {
      // Return structured errors; an uncaught throw can kill the stdio loop.
      return ['success' => FALSE, 'error' => $e->getMessage()];
    }
  }

  // Access intent — declared but NOT auto-enforced (see below).
  public function checkAccess(AccountInterface $account): AccessResultInterface {
    return AccessResult::allowedIfHasPermission($account, 'update notes via mcp');
  }

}
```

Key points:

- Attribute: `#[Tool(id, label, description, inputSchema, ...)]` from `Drupal\mcp_server\Attribute\Tool`. `id` is the MCP wire name (no `:`). Schemas live in the attribute — the base class accessors return empty. The `readOnly`/`destructive`/`idempotent`/`openWorld` flags are client hints only.
- Base class: `Drupal\mcp_server\Plugin\ToolPluginBase` (implements `ToolPluginInterface` + `ContainerFactoryPluginInterface`; injects `current_user` via `create()`).
- Handler signature (0.6 API): `public function execute(array $arguments, ClientGateway $gateway): mixed` — `$gateway` is `Mcp\Server\ClientGateway` (client sampling; most tools ignore it). `$arguments` is already validated against `inputSchema`.
- Gating — the #1 gotcha: `checkAccess()` is declared but nothing in the `tools/call` path calls it. Core ships zero auth policy, so overriding it alone does nothing. To enforce it, register an event subscriber on the SDK's `Mcp\Event\RequestEvent` that, for each `CallToolRequest`, resolves the plugin by wire name and throws `Drupal\mcp_server\Exception\McpAuthorizationDeniedException('forbidden', 403)` when `$plugin->checkToolAccess($id, $currentUser)` denies (the `ToolAccessSubscriber` pattern; register with the `event_subscriber` tag, args `@plugin.manager.mcp_server.tool`, `@current_user`). Alternatively enable the `mcp_server_oauth` companion for scope-based gating. Ideally also filter `ListToolsRequest` so denied tools don't leak into `tools/list`.
- After adding the plugin, run `drush cr` — discovery is attribute-cached.

