```php
<?php

namespace Drupal\my_module\Plugin\McpServer\Tool;

use Drupal\mcp_server\Attribute\Tool;
use Drupal\mcp_server\Plugin\ToolPluginBase;
use Mcp\Types\CallToolResult;
use Mcp\Types\TextContent;

/**
 * Creates a node title update. Write-capable, so not read-only.
 */
#[Tool(
  id: 'my_module_update_title',
  name: 'update_node_title',
  description: 'Update the title of a node.',
  // JSON Schema for arguments.
  inputSchema: [
    'type' => 'object',
    'properties' => [
      'nid' => ['type' => 'integer'],
      'title' => ['type' => 'string'],
    ],
    'required' => ['nid', 'title'],
  ],
  // MCP tool annotations: declare this tool mutates state.
  annotations: [
    'readOnlyHint' => FALSE,
    'destructiveHint' => FALSE,
    'idempotentHint' => TRUE,
  ],
)]
class UpdateNodeTitle extends ToolPluginBase {

  /**
   * mcp/sdk 0.6: arguments in, CallToolResult out.
   */
  public function execute(array $arguments): CallToolResult {
    $node = \Drupal::entityTypeManager()->getStorage('node')
      ->load($arguments['nid']);
    if (!$node || !$node->access('update')) {
      return new CallToolResult(
        content: [new TextContent(text: 'Access denied or not found.')],
        isError: TRUE,
      );
    }
    $node->setTitle($arguments['title'])->save();
    return new CallToolResult(
      content: [new TextContent(text: "Updated node {$arguments['nid']}.")],
    );
  }

}
```

Access gating happens at three layers:

1. **Route/permission layer** — the MCP endpoint itself is permission-gated (e.g. "access mcp server"); the request runs as the authenticated Drupal user (Basic auth/OAuth), so the tool executes with that account's privileges.
2. **Plugin access** — the base class supports an `access()` check (return `AccessResult::allowedIfHasPermission($account, 'administer nodes')` or similar) so the tool is hidden from `tools/list` for users who can't use it.
3. **Entity access in `execute()`** — as above, always re-check `$node->access('update')` rather than trusting the caller; write tools should never bypass entity access.

Caveat: this is from memory and the mcp_server plugin API moved quickly between mcp/sdk 0.5→0.7 (e.g. exact attribute argument names, whether the result type is `CallToolResult` vs a plain array the module wraps). Verify the base class and attribute signature against the installed module before relying on the exact spellings.

